    <span class="mono-label">PILLAR</span>
    <h1>What makes fresh graduates unhireable, and how do you fix it?</h1>

    <p class="answer-capsule">Fresh graduates aren't unhireable. They're unproven. A degree certifies that you passed through an education system built to filter people, not to show an employer you can do the job. With <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/research/college-labor-market" target="_blank" rel="noopener">41.5% of recent graduates underemployed</a>, the fix is not another credential. It is proof of work: a real task an employer can evaluate before they hire you.</p>

    <div class="tldr">
      <ul>
        <li>About <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/research/college-labor-market" target="_blank" rel="noopener">41.5% of recent graduates are underemployed</a>, working jobs that do not require their degree, per the New York Fed (Q1 2026).</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.burningglassinstitute.org/research/underemployment" target="_blank" rel="noopener">52% are underemployed a year after graduating</a>, and starting out underemployed makes you 3.5 times more likely to still be underemployed a decade later (Strada and Burning Glass, <em>Talent Disrupted</em>).</li>
        <li>The single biggest protective factor is doing real work: completing an internship <a href="https://www.strada.org/reports/talent-disrupted" target="_blank" rel="noopener">lowers the chance of underemployment by 48.5%</a>.</li>
        <li>A degree proves you attended. It no longer proves you can do the job. Employers increasingly hire on demonstrated skill.</li>
        <li>The fix is proof of work: a scored piece of real work a recruiter can judge in minutes.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <section>
      <h2>Why are so many graduates unhireable right now?</h2>
      <p>Start with how the education system actually works, because the answer is not what you were told. School is not really built to teach you. It is built to filter you. You progress by waiting a year and not failing, and the grade you get along the way barely matters, because the purpose of the whole machine is to sort people, not to prepare them. A student in Singapore, a student in India, and a student in Canada move through almost the same syllabus on almost the same timeline. The thing that varies most is the teacher.</p>
      <p>For decades the degree at the end of that filter worked as a shortcut. An employer could not measure your ability directly, so they used your degree as a proxy for it. That proxy has broken. The work the world needs now, the ability to use AI well, to communicate, to ship something with a team, changes faster than any four-year curriculum can follow, and a lot of the solo desk work that graduates used to be hired into is being absorbed by AI. The New York Fed found that recent graduates now face <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/research/college-labor-market" target="_blank" rel="noopener">higher unemployment than the workforce as a whole</a>, reversing the pattern that held for generations. So you are not imagining it. The diploma did its job of getting you through the filter, and then handed you to a market that wants evidence the filter never produced.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Is it the graduate's fault, or the system's?</h2>
      <p>It is the system's, and it helps to be honest about why. A university's product is the degree, and its customers are students and their parents. In the United States that purchase is usually financed, which makes a degree behave a lot like a mortgage: you borrow money to buy an asset, then spend years paying it off. That model only works if the asset holds its value, and for most degrees it has quietly stopped doing so. The value employers pay for has shifted from the credential to the skill.</p>
      <p>Universities are not set up to fix this, because their pricing depends on scarcity rather than outcomes. A low acceptance rate raises both status and tuition, so the incentive is to stay selective, not to guarantee you can do a job at the end. None of this is a moral failing on your part. You did the thing you were told would work. The problem is that the instructions were written for a job market that no longer exists.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What actually makes a graduate hireable?</h2>
      <p>Evidence. The clearest signal in the data is that the students who avoid the underemployment trap are the ones who did real work before they needed the job. The <em>Talent Disrupted</em> analysis found that completing an internship <a href="https://www.strada.org/reports/talent-disrupted" target="_blank" rel="noopener">cuts the odds of being underemployed by almost half</a>, and that your very first job after graduating predicts where you will be ten years later. The mechanism behind that number is not the internship itself. It is the proof the internship produces: you did the work, someone evaluated it, and now there is something real to point to.</p>
      <p>That is the thing to optimize for, and you do not have to wait for an employer to grant it to you. Proof of work is a real, scoped task from the field you want to enter, completed and scored against a professional bar. A recruiter can open it and judge it in the time it takes to read a résumé bullet, except they are judging the work instead of a claim about the work. This is the core idea behind zero. You do company-shaped tasks, they get scored against the standard of strong professionals, and the result becomes a receipt a recruiter can evaluate before they ever look at your transcript.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How do you build proof before you have the job?</h2>
      <p>Pick a direction first. Not a dream job, a direction: marketing, product, data, design, whatever you would take an entry-level role in today. Then do the actual work of that role on a real, narrow problem. A positioning brief. A product spec with trade-offs and success metrics. A short analysis that ends in a recommendation. The point is to produce the kind of artifact a working professional produces, then get it evaluated honestly so you know where it stands and what to fix.</p>
      <p>One thing we learned building zero is worth passing on. In a Middle East university pilot in Spring 2026, the students who stuck with it were not the ones we handed the most guidance to. The heavy, hold-your-hand tutorial that works in education products actively got in the way here. Students wanted to do something real within the first ninety seconds, and the thing that brought them back was specific feedback on the work they had just submitted, not another article telling them what to study. So make your loop short: do a real piece of work, get real feedback on that specific piece, fix it, do the next one. That loop builds proof faster than any amount of reading about the job.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Does proof of work replace the degree entirely?</h2>
      <p>Not yet, and you should be clear-eyed about that. Some employers still screen for a degree, and some prestigious names will keep their pull the way a luxury badge does, regardless of what is objectively better. But the direction is set. As soon as students can show employers they can do the work, and employers can hire on that evidence directly, the degree stops being the gate and becomes one signal among several. You do not have to bet on when that tips. You just have to make sure that on the day a recruiter looks at you, there is proof in front of them and not just a promise.</p>
    </section>

